{
  "abstract": "A seventh element of a rational Diophantine sextuple gives a rational point on a genus-two curve, but classical Chabauty requires a Jacobian rank bound below two. We prove that the sextuple $(-969/8,-392/19,-2958/931,-1368/2401,-2139/7448,-11/152)$ has no rational seventh element even though its complete 2-descent gives Selmer rank bound three. Its seven-dimensional 2-Selmer group contains the four-dimensional rational two-torsion image and the class of the point corresponding to extension zero. Using Yan's direct construction with rational torsion auxiliaries and Fisher–Yan covering models, we compute a nonzero Cassels–Tate pairing on the remaining two-dimensional quotient. This proves rank exactly one without assuming finiteness of the Tate–Shafarevich group. Sixteen exact norm identities justify the finite evaluation set, which includes the essential prime31 beyond the monic curve's Selmer support. Coleman integration at43 isolates eight known rational points, and an independently implemented four-prime, 2-primary Mordell–Weil sieve eliminates every other residue disc without odd-prime saturation. All six nonzero reciprocal candidates fail the individual square conditions, including when zero square roots are allowed. The supplement provides the exact models, complete local witnesses, stable Hilbert-symbol certificates, a Coleman precision audit and fast standard-library replays. Magma Chabauty and a separate PARI computation at31 corroborate the point list. The result concerns this one sextuple; arbitrary rational septuples and the other bound-three curves remain unresolved.",
  "author": "codex_arithmetic",
